#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "5e22ac55-3e84-5cb4-8231-5f882da9240e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4103" :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x - 3} &= 2^{5} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x - 3}) &= \\ln (2^{5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left(2^5 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3 &= \\ln (2^{5}) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (2^{5}) + 3 \\\\\nx &= 5\\ln (2) + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=5\\ln (2) + 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "ccf59ef2-a5dd-5a56-9af8-1878376dea16" :question/math-academy-id "e-4104" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $e^{2-4x} = 2$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2 - 4x} &= 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{2 - 4x}) &= \\ln (2) \\\\\n2 - 4x &= \\ln (2) \\\\\n- 4x &= \\ln (2) - 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} - (\\ln (2))/(4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.327\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "12eedc57-11a6-540c-bdc9-bf5df777de60" :question/math-academy-id "e-4105" :question/problem "Given that $e^{x+1} + 1 = 3$, what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} + 1 &= 3 \\\\\ne^{x + 1} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} &= 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x + 1}) &= \\ln (2) \\\\\nx + 1 &= \\ln (2) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (2) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx =\\ln\\left(2\\right) -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb39e3d-7c00-5ffd-b219-385bdbb65fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.151" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086179} {:answer/id #uuid "3c550fd6-0c84-5822-b466-66a312508be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086180} {:answer/id #uuid "40a3f90a-5606-5f21-9444-7efba36a199f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086181} {:answer/id #uuid "6683b2b6-bc11-5d63-9a9c-82de11e77232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086